\f1\fs22 Eloise's office existed in that special category of architectural intimidation where even the furniture judged you. Located in the R&D labs beneath Ruined Castle Gully, it commanded views of absolutely nothing, which Eloise claimed helped her focus on what mattered: acquiring power and occasionally remembering she had a son.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah sat across from the mahogany desk that probably cost more than most people's organs, trying not to vomit. Little Clinton had discovered her bladder made an excellent trampoline. The Johnny lounged in the chair beside her, radiating the kind of confidence only available to men who'd destroyed entire laboratories and still expected promotions.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"We need more discs," Eloise announced, as if declaring war on a small nation. Which, Sarah reflected, wasn't far off. "Five, to be precise. Johnny's modeling suggests a pentagonal configuration will stabilize the resonance."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"My modeling," The Johnny interjected, examining his manicure, "is revolutionary. I've essentially solved physics."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You essentially solved how to turn a research facility into a crater," Sarah muttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Creative destruction. Very avant-garde." He flashed his teeth, which had definitely been whitened since his termination. Apparently, unemployment agreed with him. "The point is, we need more discs. For science. And glory. Mostly glory."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise's smile could have frosted glass. "The question is acquisition. As we now know, thanks to dear Hayley's explosive curiosity, every stasis unit in the country contains a Gyges Disc. The entire refrigeration industry is built on reality-warping technology that Fridge Force has been hiding since\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Since some idiot figured out time-stopping vegetables was more profitable than understanding the fundamental nature of existence," Sarah finished. "Yes, we know. We also know Fridge Force executes people for looking at stasis units wrong. Remember the Chuglinghams?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"They're fine. Living here now, actually. Lovely people. Their son makes excellent samosas." Eloise waved dismissively. "The point is, we need discs without triggering what I believe Fridge Force calls 'Total Refrigeration Protocol.'"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny sat forward, his expression taking on the particular gleam of someone about to suggest something catastrophically stupid. "We could simply take them. I have experience with breaking and entering. Well, entering. The breaking usually happens afterward."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"From where?" Sarah asked, though she suspected she wouldn't like the answer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Empty Airborne mansions," Eloise said, producing a tablet showing real estate listings that looked like pornography for the wealthy. "The mountains are full of vacation homes that sit empty fifty weeks a year. Their owners are too rich to visit and too paranoid to sell. They just sit there, preserved in stasis-controlled perfection."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"With security systems that make PAPER look friendly," Sarah pointed out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Details." The Johnny waved his hand like he was dismissing a waiter. "I'm sure between your... what do you call it? Leadership? And my genius, we can figure something out."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise leaned back, her chair adjusting with a whisper that probably cost extra. "My people could handle it, naturally. But I prefer to maintain plausible deniability. Fridge Force can be so touchy about their monopoly on reality manipulation."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"So get someone else to do it," Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Precisely my thought." Eloise's smile widened. "The Elvi."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"No."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"They're surprisingly competent when properly motivated. And you're their Holy Momma. One word from you\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I said no." Sarah's hand moved protectively to her stomach. "They're fighting a civil war. Half of them are dying from the TMinus virus. The other half are too busy with the Great Humperdicking to\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The great what now?" The Johnny looked genuinely confused, which for him was like a resting state.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Never mind. The point is, I'm not dragging the Elvi into this."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise sighed, the sound of a predator pretending to be disappointed. "Sarah, darling. Do you remember what happened the last time you refused me?" She touched her tablet, and the wall screen displayed security footage of Eloise's last visit to Sarah's mansion. "That poor man from accounting. What was his name? The one I had shot to make a point?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Gerald Henderson."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Was it? They all blur together. Middle management is so interchangeable." She swiped, and more footage appeared. "I have forty-three international dentist assassins on speed dial. Well, forty-two. One decided to pursue his true passion in orthodontics. Very disappointing."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah felt Little Clinton perform what might have been either a backflip or an attempt to burrow through her spine. "You wouldn't."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Wouldn't I? Do you know how hard it is to find good dental assassins? They have to maintain their licenses in multiple countries. The continuing education alone is murderous." Eloise examined her nails. "I could start with the Elvi Parliament. Work my way down. Maybe have them floss a few legislators to death. Very painful. Excellent for gum health, terrible for survival."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"That's\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Or perhaps young Tony? I understand he's developed quite the following. The Great Teacher, they call him. Shame if he accidentally got a root canal. The kind that goes all the way through."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny shifted uncomfortably. "Maybe we should consider alternatives\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Shut up, Johnny," both women said simultaneously.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stood, her fury overriding the various complaints from her internal construction project. "You can threaten me all you want. Execute your entire dental death squad. I don't care. But I will not drag the Elvi into this. They've been property, they've been infected, they've been divided. I won't make them thieves too."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Such nobility." Eloise's voice could have frozen hell and charged it rent. "Very touching. Completely impractical, but touching."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I don't care."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You will." Eloise touched her tablet again. "Johnny, be a dear and show Sarah out. She looks ready to give birth all over my Persian rug. It's seventeenth century. The clash would be unbearable."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stormed toward the door, each step a small act of defiance against physics and pregnancy. "This isn't over."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Of course not. It's barely begun." Eloise's voice followed her. "Do let me know when you change your mind. Or when you run out of Elvi to protect. Whichever comes first."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah slammed the door hard enough to make the reality-warping technology in the building's stasis units hiccup. Somewhere, a refrigerated sandwich briefly existed in two timelines simultaneously.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny caught up with her in the corridor, his cologne preceding him like an aromatic herald. "That went well."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Shut up, Johnny."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I'm just saying, if you need someone to coordinate with the Elvi\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah whirled on him, pregnancy be damned. "If you so much as breathe in their direction, I'll personally ensure your next experiment involves discovering how many Gyges Discs fit in your colon."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny's smile faltered, then reformed like a cockroach surviving nuclear winter. "Noted. Though that would make an interesting research paper."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah left him there, contemplating his own posterior possibilities. Behind them, in her office, Eloise was already making calls. The game was changing, and Sarah had just made herself a player rather than a piece.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The question was whether she'd survive long enough to matter.\

